problem_statement:
Issue summary
When two base classes each have class-level pytest markers (e.g. @pytest.mark.foo on class Foo and @pytest.mark.bar on class Bar), a subclass that inherits from both (class TestDings(Foo, Bar)) only receives the markers from the first base according to Python's attribute resolution (MRO). As a result, markers from the other base class are lost for the subclass tests.

This behavior is visible across multiple pytest versions (3–6) and is consistent with standard attribute lookup semantics: pytestmark is just a class attribute, so the attribute found on lookup is the one from the first base class in MRO. However, it's arguably more intuitive for pytest to merge markers from all base classes (deduplicating by name) so that test methods inherit all relevant markers from the entire inheritance chain.

A practical workaround is to use a metaclass that exposes a pytestmark property which merges the _pytestmark lists from the class and its MRO. But that requires extra user code; it would be nicer if pytest handled this merging automatically or documented the intended behavior.

Minimal illustrative example (concept)
- Foo has @pytest.mark.foo
- Bar has @pytest.mark.bar
- class TestDings(Foo, Bar) defines a test method test_dings
Expected: test_dings is marked with both foo and bar.
Actual: test_dings only has the marker from the first base class in MRO (foo), unless a metaclass is used to merge markers.

Tentative Reproducing Test
# Starting point test to demonstrate the issue; not a final solution.
# Save this as test_marker_inheritance.py and run with pytest to observe the behavior.

import itertools
import pytest

def _marker_names(item):
    # Return a list of marker names applied to the given test item at runtime.
    return [m.name for m in item.iter_markers()]

def test_without_metaclass_loses_one_marker(request):
    @pytest.mark.foo
    class Foo:
        pass

    @pytest.mark.bar
    class Bar:
        pass

    class TestDings(Foo, Bar):
        def test_dings(self):
            # This test is expected to have both 'foo' and 'bar' markers.
            # In current pytest behavior (attribute lookup via MRO), only the
            # marker from the first base (Foo) is observed.
